title: karr log --last 0 removes the bound; --last -N reports an empty log, exit 0
updated: 2026-08-13T02:21:26Z
---

Found in a bug hunt; reproduced and re-verified independently.

Cmd/Log.pm:65-70 and :109-111 apply the limit behind a truthiness guard,

    if ($self->last && @entries > $self->last)

with no range check on the option. 0 is falsy, so the one option whose job
is to bound the output silently removes the bound. A negative passes the
guard and slices @entries[-(-N) .. -1], i.e. N .. -1 -- the empty list -- so
the command reports an empty log and exits 0.

This is ticket #76, already fixed twice elsewhere in the same tree:
Show.pm:161-162 raises a usage error for --last < 1, and Context.pm:97-99
does the same for --activity-limit, with a comment spelling out this exact
failure ("the falsy guard reads 0 as 'no bound at all' ... and a negative
produced an empty section and exit 0, indistinguishable from 'nobody else
acted'"). Cmd::Log carries the identical option and never got the fix.

Reproduction (board with 6 log entries):

    karr log --last 3   ->  3 lines   (correct)
    karr log --last 0   ->  6 lines   (bound silently removed)

    $ karr log --last -3
    No log entries.                    exit=0
    $ karr log --last -3 --json
    []                                 exit=0

    $ karr show --last 0
    Usage error: --last must be 1 or greater (got 0)      exit=2
    $ karr context --activity-limit 0
    Usage error: --activity-limit must be 1 or greater    exit=2

Cost: `karr log --last $N` with an $N that computes to 0 or below either
dumps an unbounded log or claims the board has no activity at all -- both
with exit 0, so a script cannot tell the difference.

Fix is the rule already written down twice: usage error for < 1, per ADR 0002.
